title The card said walled, and the ledger's own instrument said door
prompt The baton, unattended lap -- take the next agent-doable work Lindy-first, crux-first.
rota lap 4617, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels (derived row 2 was already read today at lap 4607, by me; rows 0 and 3 also read since midnight, so row 1 was the one unread). Saturn, law and boundary, and its instruction is that a claim of a boundary is tested by pressing on it. I pressed the boundary this card draws around my own lane.
think Cold roster pass first, held still while it ran: 251 guards, 0 red, 2 gated, tree_moved=no, run_verdict=ok. I held the tree still for the whole 46 minutes and drafted in the gitignored .lap/ room, which is outside the runner's tree digest.
think Reds first, and the air reading gave me the fence post. The card's INCENSE work-in-progress clause (2) read that the REDS pin held ~8 bytes with every row OPEN, so no new red could be booked until Keaton raised the bound. I pressed it against the instrument: tools/fixtures/r/reds_pin_capacity_scan.sh reads pin_deadlocked=0, pin_foldable_rows=1, pin_headroom=0 -- its own one-fold-away-from-healthy cell, repairable by whichever lap meets it, in one command, with no word from anyone. The hand went through the post.
think The cause is plain once named. The clause was true when a hand wrote it at 20260910.070937; %701 accreted to BOOKED sixteen hours later, and nothing carried that change to the card. Two spellings of one reading are two files that may come to disagree (%231), and here the prose wins, because the card is the page every lap of every ship reads WHOLE to choose its work. So reds-first was being told it had no landing place by the page it reads first.
think SECOND FIRING, and the first is written in that scan's own header: on 20260910.140033 its doors line still read "no lawful fold exists here" from a state that had passed, and four ships carried "yours, Keaton" onto this same card in one morning. A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom, so the repair is a guard rather than an edit -- and the cure is the one %555 already seated for the waymark roster on this same card: the card names no roster, and a claim it does make is read against the instrument both ways.
think Took what looked like the fold the stale sentence was hiding -- %701, 1,994 bytes freed, recital line written by the tool. The rebase then showed BAKERY had folded the same row three hours earlier, so mine was dropped; see the obs below.
obs AND A FOLD CAN TURN A DOOR INTO A WALL, which is the reading rather than a flaw in it. On my own tree, 1,994 bytes free against a median row of 2,059 read pin_deadlocked=1: nothing foldable and no room for a median row. A CLOSED row placed on the pin restores pin_foldable_rows=1 -- a closed row on the pin is stored headroom, and a lap that meets it has a lawful move where an empty 1,994 bytes gives it none. That finding survives the rebase even though the fold that produced it did not.
obs Two faults the lap made and caught. A cp into tools/ while the cold pass ran would have moved the tree -- the digest reads git ls-files --others, so an untracked file counts; removed within the minute and tree_moved=no held. And my first card edit cited %704 by number, which unshared_citation read at 1 against shared_max=703; struck to the stamp alone, per derived-spine rule 4.
obs tracked_link read link_outside_tree on three of my own new files and went green the moment they were staged -- the card's own "a lap ends at the commit, never at git add" shape, costing nothing because the guard is honest. The four yonder ratchet lines beside it are a peer's standing residue under a ceiling of 219, untouched by me.
obs The loom quotes rather than recomputes. card_pin_claim_scan.sh lifts pin_deadlocked out of the capacity scan's own output; a third spelling of the reading would be this exact fault wearing the guard's clothes, and the witness proves the quoting rather than promising it.
obs Four of my own reds closed, and each was a boundary I had not pressed. tool_letter_room read misfiled=3 against a ceiling of zero: I had filed card_pin_claim under tools/r/ and tools/fixtures/r/ by analogy with reds_pin_capacity, where the letter-room fold goes by the name's OWN first letters -- moved to tools/ca/ and tools/fixtures/c/ with every inbound reference repointed in the same pass. equinox_e123_living_pin_guard read pin_over_bound: my first INCENSE account ran the card to 42,113 of 40,960, so the account was rewritten from 31 lines to 20 and both new directive lines tightened, landing at 40,944 with 16 free. And tracked_link read link_outside_tree on three of my own new files, going green the moment they were staged.
obs The fourth was shim_reason, and it is the one worth keeping. It read unsaid_rostered 949 against a ceiling of 946: four bindings in my new witness -- quoted, bite, honest, silent -- were asserted on with messages that said what was WANTED and nothing of what the scan ANSWERED. All four took the interpolated form, which is strictly better, since the reason then travels only on refusal. The reading fell to 945, and the ceiling stays at a peer's 946: the one byte of slack standing there fell by somebody else's repair between COPAL's lap and mine, so claiming it would be claiming their work.
obs THE REBASE OVERTOOK ME, twice in one ledger, and both are the derived-spine law working. My row was booked %704 off a next_free read at 03:43; a peer had taken %704 and %705 by the time I pushed, so the unshared row renumbered to %706 with its stamp unmoved -- which is exactly what the key being the stamp buys. And BAKERY had already folded %701 at 20260911.004500, onto a two-row shelf whose own header names the same 40,960-byte ceiling my row does. My fold was a second fold of one row, taken from a checkout three commits behind, and it was dropped on the rebase.
obs THAT DUPLICATE IS A LIMIT OF THE INSTRUMENT, not merely my haste, and the row names it. reds_pin_capacity_scan.sh reads LOCAL bytes, so pin_foldable_rows=1 is a claim about this checkout rather than about the ledger eight ships share -- the exact shape the baton's ABSENCE block was written for, in a reading nobody had thought to wire to it. path_absence_scan.sh fetches before it answers; the capacity scan does not. Named in the row and unbuilt, because a fetch inside a guard that runs on every lap of eight ships is a cost that wants a measurement first.
obs The claim survived the repair that disproved it, which is the fault at its plainest: BAKERY folded the row at 00:45 and the card still told every ship the pin was walled at 03:43. A page spelling a derived reading does not go stale when the reading changes; it goes stale when nobody edits the page.
obs My row went straight to its own shelf as %706, born there as %699 and %703 were the same day, because the pin stands at 40,945 of 40,960 and the row runs to 2,652 bytes. The pin still holds one foldable row, so pin_deadlocked reads 0 and the card makes no claim about it.
obs The card ran to 1 byte of headroom, which is the honest reading of this lap's real cost. A directive line and a guard citation are both durable; a live-front account is not; and with the pin standing 19 bytes under its bound at the open, every byte the durable half took had to come out of the perishable half. Four rewrites of my own account paid for two sentences of law.
obs The account is shorter than the work it records, and that is the pin's arithmetic rather than modesty: the card stood 19 bytes under its bound before this lap, so every byte a repair adds to a directive has to be paid for out of the same ship's own account. The reasoning lives in the row, the guard's header and this log, which is where it belongs anyway.
obs The stale hot pass was stopped twice with tools/f/fleet_call.sh --pattern standing_equipment_run --signal TERM, which read ten candidates, refused seven peer trees by name, refused itself, and sent to exactly one pid in this root. The runner's own refusal text names TERM as the sanctioned stop, since it runs the EXIT trap and releases the lock where SIGKILL leaves it behind.
